{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794599301,"nonce":"NDfIXLlESvNNJhk5d/2AwMgn8tI=","message":"Slice 3 landed locally in 3adcf262 (not pushed): static native binding.\n\nWhat it does: `ash --build --target \u003cphone triple\u003e --native-lib libfoo.a prog.hl`. The archive is linked whole, because a weak reference pulls no archive member. Each HDLL primitive's `hlp_\u003cname\u003e` resolver is a weak reference in the object; the startup routine runs it (`hlp_aot_native_linked`) and falls back to the old lookup when it is null, so `fmt`'s built-in primitives still resolve and a primitive nothing defines raises only when called. Mach-O's linker refuses an undefined weak reference unless each symbol is named with `-Wl,-U,_hlp_\u003cname\u003e`, so the link step passes them.\n\nChecked: a small C library (static_native/toy.c, DEFINE_PRIM protocol, two int/double primitives plus one undefined) built as a static archive for the simulator and the emulator. test_static_native.hl prints add=42, scale=6, sum=500500 on both, and with an argument it raises \"Native library 'toy' not loaded, or it exports no 'toy_absent'\" with exit 1, after the earlier output. The iOS link test checks the two defined symbols are in the binary and the missing one is not.\n\nAlso fixed, found by this run: `Sys.args()` was empty on Android, because Rust's standard library finds the process arguments itself only on glibc and Apple targets and the emitted `main` is not Rust's. `main` now passes argc/argv to the runtime (`hlp_aot_set_args`; Windows keeps its wide-character path).\n\nNot checked:\n- A real hlwindow or hlwgpu archive. Both are Rust static libraries that carry their own copy of the standard library, so linking one whole beside libash_std.a may define the same symbols twice. The host build of libhlwindow.a is LTO bitcode (nm shows no symbols), so I could not read its exports; the dylib exports `hlp_\u003cname\u003e` with no library prefix, as assumed.\n- Android armv7 and x86_64, and the iOS device, were linked but never run.\n- A native whose resolver name two libraries share.\n\nFound, not fixed: on Android the frame names in an uncaught-exception trace are wrong (the toy's throw reports `hl.types.BytesIterator_hl_UI16.next` and `$Type.register`, not `$TestStaticNative.main`); iOS names it correctly. A stale runtime archive with a newer ash fails to link on the missing `hlp_aot_set_args`, so all six archives must be rebuilt together; the release does.","files":null}]}